('Most α-particles pass through thin foil almost undeflected because atoms are mostly empty space. Rare large-angle deflections show positive charge and most mass concentrated in a very small nucleus.','Why do most α-particles pass through foil?','What does a rare large-angle α deflection show?'),
('A nuclear atom has a small nucleus containing protons and neutrons, with electrons outside the nucleus in orbitals. A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons.','Where are protons and neutrons in an atom?','Where are orbital electrons?'),
('Proton number $Z$ counts protons; nucleon number $A$ counts protons plus neutrons. Neutron number is $A-Z$.','What does proton number count?','How is neutron number found from $A$ and $Z$?'),
('Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same proton number but different numbers of neutrons; their nucleon numbers differ.','What is common to isotopes?','What differs between isotopes?'),
('In ${}^{A}_{Z}X$, $X$ is the element symbol, $A$ is nucleon number and $Z$ is proton number. The nucleus contains $A-Z$ neutrons.','What does the upper number in nuclide notation mean?','What does the lower number mean?'),
('In any nuclear process, the total nucleon number and total electric charge are conserved across all reactants and products.','Which two quantities balance in a nuclear equation?','Why include every emitted particle when balancing?'),
